      "level": 3,
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "1. What does orientation mean?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Orientation means perspective or point of view. It describes how a person looks at or understands a given thing.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "2. What is object orientation?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Object orientation is the process of looking at and treating real-world entities as objects.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "3. What is object perspective?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Object perspective means viewing a person, place, thing, or concept as an object that can be represented in software.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "For example, a stylus should be viewed as an object rather than only as a writing tool.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "4. How should a Java developer view the real world?",
